
\section{The Tableau}\label{sec:tableau}


We decide satisfiability of an input formula $\varphi$ by a depth-first, on-the-fly construction that builds
a graph of symbolic nodes and searches it for a {successful marking}. A node carries the
formulas to be realised at a configuration; zones represent clock valuations symbolically; and a single
{cover} rule discharges every time-bounded modality. We fix nodes and realisability (\S\ref{ss:nodes}), give the rules (\S\ref{ss:rules}), define
acceptance and its test (\S\ref{ss:loop}), and close with two examples (\S\ref{ss:ex}).

\subsection{Nodes and Realisability}\label{ss:nodes}


In our tableau, a {node} is a tuple $N=\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}$, where $\Gamma$ is the set of
goal formulas (refer to as \emph{demands}) to realise at the current configuration; $\Ctx$ the {context} of universal formulas that must
hold at every successor; $Z$ the set of clock valuations for this node, represented as a canonical zone; $\obligations$ the {clock map}, which allocates to every active timed modality $\mu$ a dedicated formula-clock $x_\mu$ so its window (i.e. its interval \kI) can be measured from the configuration that demanded it; and $D$ is a set of deadlines
$(x_\mu,\sup\kI)$, each with a strictness flag, and $\Inv(N)=\bigwedge_{(x_\mu,b)\in D}x_\mu\le b$
its induced invariant.

%A deadline is added when \ruleref{Cov} launches the pursuit of a
%universal until and removed when the $(\text{stop})$ branch of \ruleref{Uunf} discharges it;
%no other rule writes $D$.
%
%; and $\eventualities$ the postponed eventualities used for loopacceptance. 


%Write $\Gamma_{\mathsf{prop}}=\Gamma\cap\big(\mathcal P\cup\{\neg p\mid p\in\mathcal P\}\big)$ for the {propositional part} of $\Gamma$, the atomic literals it asserts. A node is {closed} if $\Gamma_{\mathsf{prop}}$ is propositionally inconsistent, i.e., it contains a complementary pair $p,\neg p$ (equivalently $\bot\in\Gamma$), or its zone is empty, $Z=\emptyset$; it is {saturated} when no expansion rule applies.



\noindent
We write
$\Gamma_{\mathsf{prop}}=\Gamma\cap\big(\mathcal P\cup\{\neg p\mid p\in\mathcal P\}\big)$ for the
\emph{propositional part} of $\Gamma$, the atomic literals it asserts. 

We call a sequence of nodes $N_0,N_1,\dots$ a \emph{path} if each $N_{i+1}$ is a child of $N_i$. 
%Write
%\[
%  \Inv(N)\hspace{-0.1cm}=\hspace{-0.1cm}\bigwedge\big\{x_\mu\le\sup\kI \big| \mu=\mathrm{A}(\varphi\,\mathbf{U}_\kI\,\psi)
%  \text{ under pursuit at } N \big\} \quad (<\text{ if } \sup\kI\notin\kI)
%\]
%for the node's {invariant}. Both $x_\mu$ and the bound are read off the node: the clock
%from $\obligations$, the interval from $\mu$ itself.
%
A node is \emph{closed} if $\Gamma_{\mathsf{prop}}$ is propositionally inconsistent, i.e. it contains a
complementary pair $p,\neg p$ (equivalently $\bot\in\Gamma$), or its zone is empty, $Z=\emptyset$. It is
\emph{saturated} when no saturation or cover rule applies; only saturated nodes generate successors.
It is \emph{realisable} if there is a non-blocking TTS $\mathcal T$ induced by a TA, a location $s$ with
$L(s)\supseteq\Gamma_{\mathsf{prop}}$ and $\Inv(s)=\Inv(N)$, and an interpretation of the
formula-clocks in $\clocks(\obligations)$, such that for every $\nu \in Z$ the configuration $(s,\nu)$ such that  $\mathcal T,(s,\nu)\models\textstyle\bigwedge\Gamma\ \wedge\ \bigwedge\Ctx$. Realisability is \emph{monotone under narrowing}: if $\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}$ is realisable and
$\emptyset\neq Z'\subseteq Z$, then $\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z'}{\obligations}{D}$ is realisable.


%Two consequences of Definition~\ref{def:realisable} shape the calculus. First, the quantification over $Z$ is \emph{universal}: this is precisely what lets a single node discharge a demand re-imposed by a persistent context at a continuum of onsets. Second, realisability is \emph{monotone under narrowing}.

