\subsection{Rules}\label{ss:rules}


Each rule rewrites a node into its children, shown on the full tuple; a component reappears
unchanged unless the rule modifies it. The rules come in four groups: \emph{saturation}
decomposes the goals in $\Gamma$, the \emph{cover} rule discharges a timed modality, the
\emph{pursuit} rules unfold a launched modality one step at a time, and \emph{successor
generation} expands a saturated node in time and across edges.
\paragraph{Saturation.}
Conjunction \ruleref{Conj} is deterministic, disjunction \ruleref{Disj} branches, and \ruleref{AX} handles a universal marker by moving its argument into the context. %There is no saturation rule for $\EX$: ``some successor satisfies
%$\psi$'' cannot be settled before successors exist, so $\EX\psi$ waits in $\Gamma$ and is discharged by
%\ruleref{EX}.
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\[
\ \frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi\land\psi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi,\psi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}\quad
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Conj}\\\DETn}
\qquad
\ \frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\AX\varphi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx\cup\{\varphi\}}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}\quad
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{AX}\\ \DETn}
\]\vspace{-0.35cm}
\[
\ \frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi\lor\psi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}\quad\big|\quad
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\psi\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
\quad
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Disj}\\\ORn}
\]

\paragraph{The cover rule.}
When $\Gamma$ contains a timed modality $\mu=\mathrm{Q}(\varphi\,\circ_\kI\,\psi)$, with
$\mathrm{Q}\in\{\mathrm{E},\mathrm{A}\}$ and $\circ\in\{\mathbf{U},\mathbf{R}\}$, we say the node
\emph{demands} $\mu$: by realisability it must be shown that $\mu$ holds at every
valuation of the node's zone. Since $\kI$ is measured from the demanding configuration, a persistent
context such as $\mathrm{AG}(\mu)$ would issue a new demand at uncountably many configurations of the
zone; we avoid this by testing the zone symbolically. Suppose we knew the set $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu) \subseteq \mathbb{R}_{\ge0}^{\clockSet}$ of all clock valuations at which $\mu$ holds under the current context. The demand is then met exactly when the node's zone lies totally inside it, $Z\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$, discharged by \ruleref{Cov}.
%The rule
%\ruleref{Cov} discharges $\mu$ when the cover is total, \ruleref{Covstar} narrows the node when it is
%partial, and the node closes when it is empty.
%
\vspace{-0.3cm}
\[
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{discharge})\\[3pt]
\tup{\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\rst{Z}{x_\mu}}{\obligations[\mu\mapsto x_\mu]}{D\cup D_\mu}
 & (\text{launch})
\end{array}}
\quad
\begin{array}{l}
Z\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)\\
x_\mu \text{ fresh}
\end{array}
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Cov}\\\ANDn}
\]

The cover rule applies a deadline $D_\mu=\{(x_\mu,\sup\kI)\}$ iff $\mu$ is a universal until,
$\mu=\mathrm{A}(\varphi\,\mathbf{U}_\kI\,\psi)$, and otherwise $D_\mu=\emptyset$. Only such a
modality needs one, as it obliges \emph{every} run to satisfy $\psi$ within $\kI$: time must be
forced forward or a run could idle past the window with the eventuality unmet. An existential
until is discharged by a single witnessing run, and a release, of either quantifier, is
discharged by lapsing past the window rather than by acting within it.




%The universal deferral is additionally capped at the deadline $x_\mu\le\sup\kI$ (strict when
%$\sup\kI\notin\kI$). We record that cap in $\Inv(N)$ rather than in $Z$: a deadline restricts \emph{how
%long time may elapse} while the pursuit is live, which is exactly the semantics of a location invariant,
%and it must survive to the extracted automaton as one.
%
%
%The asymmetry with $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$ is
%deliberate. For $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$ the cap is load-bearing: it is what \emph{forces} every run into
%the window, so that no branch may drift past it with the eventuality unmet. 

%\[
%\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
%{\begin{array}{ll}
%\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{discharge})\\[3pt]
%\tup{\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\rst{Z}{x_\mu}}{\obligations[\mu\mapsto x_\mu]}{D\cup D_\mu}
% & (\text{launch})
%\end{array}}
%\quad
%\begin{array}{l}
%Z\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu),\quad x_\mu \text{ fresh}\\
%D_\mu=\{(x_\mu,\sup\kI)\}\text{ if }\mu=\mathrm{A}(\varphi\,\mathbf{U}_\kI\,\psi),\\
%D_\mu=\emptyset\text{ otherwise}
%\end{array}
%\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Cov}\\\ANDn}
%\]


The two children are conjunctive because they play different roles: the
\emph{stop} settles $\mu$ at once, allowing the node to continue with the rest of $\Gamma$, while the \emph{go} child pursues $\mu$, which we indicate with $\mu^\bullet$. A pursuit of $\mu$ starts by introducing a fresh formula-clock $x_\mu$ and resetting it to zero, so that the window $\kI$ can be measured from the demanding configuration. 

When the cover is partial, the node narrows to a convex component of the covered part (sound by monotonicity of realisability under narrowing) and is an OR-choice, since it suffices to build a model over one component.
\vspace{-0.1cm}
\[
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu\}}{\Ctx}{Z_i}{\obligations}{D}}
\quad
\begin{array}{l}
Z\not\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu),\quad Z\cap\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)\neq\emptyset\\
Z_i\in\cvx\big(Z\cap\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)\big)
\end{array}
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Covstar}\\\ORn}
\]
where $\cvx(\cdot)$ denotes the finite set of convex components of a finite union of zones, each of which
is itself a zone and hence representable as a single DBM. If $Z\cap\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)=\emptyset$ neither
rule applies and the node is closed. Thus the cover rule turns the membership test
$Z\subseteq\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ into a proof step, with narrowing as its only escape.




\paragraph{Pursuit: Until and Release Modalities.}

In order to apply the \ruleref{Cov}, we must compute $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ for every demanded $\mu$, at the logical level without assuming a model. This is computed by \emph{pursuing} $\mu$, which unfolds the formula step by step according to specific rules depending on whether it is an until or a release modality. We first provide the unfolding rules for until and release modalities, and then explain how to compute $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ based on the unfolding.





%
%Pursuing $\mu$ 
%In addition to introducing $x_\mu$, the pursuit unfolds $\mu$ one step at a time by the rules \ruleref{Uunf}, \ruleref{Rsplit} and \ruleref{Runf}. 
%
%A step produces two kinds of children. A \emph{base} child settles the demand at once and drops $\mu^\bullet$. A \emph{recursive} child defers it, passing $\nxQ\mu^\bullet$ to a successor
%($\EX\mu^\bullet$ into $\Gamma$ if $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$, $\AX\mu^\bullet$ into the context if
%$\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$), where the pursuit resumes; there $x_\mu$ keeps running, across delays as well
%as edges. 



Let us first consider an until modality $\mu=\mathrm{Q}(\varphi\,\mathbf{U}_\kI\,\psi)$.  The unfolding of $\mu$ is a disjunction between two cases: either $\psi$ holds now, in which case the demand is discharged, or $\varphi$ holds now and the pursuit continues. \vspace{-0.1cm}
%An until stops when it reaches $\psi$ inside the window, and otherwise goes, asserting $\varphi$ and deferring. The rule has exactly two children; the second takes one of two forms according to $\mathrm{Q}$.
\[
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\psi\}}{\Ctx}{Z\cap(x_\mu\!\in\!\kI)}{\obligations}{D\setminus\{(x_\mu,\sup\kI)\}}
  & (\text{stop})\\[4pt]
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi,\EX\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}
  & \text{if } \mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E},\\[3pt]
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi\}}{\Ctx\cup\{\AX\mu^\bullet\}}{Z}{\obligations}{D}
  & \text{if } \mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A},
\end{array}
\right.
& (\text{go})
\end{array}}
\quad
\mu=\mathrm{Q}(\varphi\mathbf{U}_\kI\,\psi)
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Uunf}\\\ORn
}
\]

In the discharge branch, the deadline is removed from $D$ because the pursuit has been discharged. 






%%%% Maybe adding it in the loop part later
%For $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$
%no cap is needed, because $\mu$ was placed in $\eventualities$ at launch and a run that defers forever
%yields a non-fulfilling loop, rejected by \S\ref{ss:loop}; adding the cap there would only prune, never
%change the verdict.
%

Consider now a release modality $\mu=\mathrm{Q}(\varphi\,\mathbf{R}_\kI\,\psi)$. The semantics of release imposes that $\psi$ must hold at every point in the window, unless $\varphi$ releases it. We handle the pursuit of a release in two stages. First, we partition the zone according to the position of $x_\mu$ relative to $\kI$, and then we pursue each part separately. We write $\ZltI = Z\cap(x_\mu<\inf\kI$ or $ x_\mu=\inf\kI\notin\kI)$, $\ZinI = Z\cap(x_\mu\in\kI)$ and $\ZgtI = Z\cap(x_\mu>\sup\kI$ or $ x_\mu=\sup\kI\notin\kI)$ for the three parts of the partition,
%
%
%\begin{align*}
%    \ZltI = Z&\cap(x_\mu<\inf\kI\ \text{or}\ x_\mu=\inf\kI\notin\kI),\quad \ZinI = Z\cap(x_\mu\in\kI),\quad\\
%    &\ZgtI = Z\cap(x_\mu>\sup\kI\ \text{or}\ x_\mu=\sup\kI\notin\kI),
%\end{align*}
%
so that the strictness of the interval endpoints is carried by the DBM bounds and the three parts are a partition of $Z$. Empty parts are dropped. Accordingly, the unfolding of a release produces up to three children, one for each part of the partition. The first two are pursued, while the last is lapsed and drops $\mu^\bullet$.
\[
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{lll}
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZltI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{before})\\[2pt]
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZinI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{in})\\[2pt]
\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{\ZgtI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{after: lapsed})
\end{array}}
\quad \mu=\mathrm{Q}(\varphi\,\mathbf{R}_\kI\,\psi)
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Rsplit}\\\ANDn}
\]

The before and in parts are pursued by the following rule, which is a disjunction between two cases: either $\varphi$ is released (stop), or the pursuit continues(go). %The after part is lapsed and drops $\mu^\bullet$.
%Within the before and in parts the pursuit then either discharges through $\varphi$ or continues; this is a choice, hence an OR-node.
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\[
\scriptsize{
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZltI}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi\}}{\Ctx}{\ZltI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{stop})\\[2pt]
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\nxQ\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZltI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{go})
\end{array}}
\shortstack{
\ruleanchor{Runf_before}\\
    \ORn
}
\frac{\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZinI}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\varphi,\psi\}}{\Ctx}{\ZinI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{stop})\\[2pt]
\tup{\Gamma\cup\{\psi,\nxQ\mu^\bullet\}}{\Ctx}{\ZinI}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{go})
\end{array}}
\shortstack{
\ruleanchor{Runf_in}\\
    \ORn
}
}
\]



The unfolding of these rules produces two kinds of children. A \emph{base} child settles the demand at once and drops $\mu^\bullet$. A \emph{recursive} child defers it, passing $\nxQ\mu^\bullet$ to a successor ($\EX\mu^\bullet$ into $\Gamma$ if $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$, $\AX\mu^\bullet$ into the context if $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$), where the pursuit resumes; there $x_\mu$ keeps running, across delays as well as edges. Repeated unfolding thus allows us to compute $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$.  Let $B_\mu$ be the union of the base-case zones. A valuation lies in $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$
exactly when it lies in $B_\mu$, or matches a recursive child whose deferred successors
already do (some successor when $\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$, all of them when
$\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$). This is a monotone equation on finite unions of $\cong_M$-zones, so
it has a least and a greatest solution, each reached by finite iteration: an until takes the
least, qualifying a valuation only if it reaches a base child in finitely many steps; a
release the greatest, qualifying it as long as it never violates the obligation. The equation
refers to $\Sat_{\Ctx}$ only through modalities strictly inside $\varphi$ and $\psi$, so this
is an induction on $\FL(\varphi)$. A single $x_\mu$ therefore serves all onsets of a persistent
demand, the dense family of onsets being absorbed into one finite union of zones, and the
cover rule decides every demand against it.
%
%\begin{remark}[Stratification]\label{rem:strat}
%The definition is not circular. $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ is computed in a separate
%\emph{pursuit graph}, generated by \ruleref{Uunf}, \ruleref{Rsplit},
%\ruleref{Runf} and successor generation alone; the cover rule is never
%re-entered for $\mu$ itself. Nested modalities are resolved by induction on the
%Fischer--Ladner order: the pursuit of $\mu$ may consult $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu')$ only
%for $\mu'\in\FL(\mu)\setminus\{\mu\}$, already available. Within one stratum the
%unfolding is a monotone map on finite unions of $\cong_M$-zones, so by
%Knaster--Tarski its least and greatest fixpoints exist and are reached by finite
%iteration. In particular $\Sat_{\Ctx}(\mu)$ is a finite union of zones, and a
%single $x_\mu$ serves all onsets of a persistent demand, since the dense family
%of onsets is absorbed into that union.
%\end{remark}


\paragraph{Successor generation.}
A saturated and covered node is expanded by two rules, one elapsing time and one taking discrete steps.
%\emph{Both are AND-rules}: a model must admit the passage of time \emph{and} supply every demanded edge.
%The choice between waiting and moving is not made here --- it is made by the stop/go branching of \ruleref{Uunf} and \ruleref{Runf}, which is where the existential content of the calculus lives.

\ruleref{Delay} advances the zone to the next integer hyperplane $x=b$ ($b\le M$) of an active bound,
and does so in two steps: $\Dopen Z$ is the time-successor of $Z$ strictly below the next
hyperplane, and $\Dbd Z$ is that hyperplane (equivalently $\uparrow\!Z$ when no hyperplane remains at or
below $M$). %Both are computed under the node's invariant, which is where $\Inv(N)$ bounds the elapse:
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\[
 \frac{\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\begin{array}{ll}
\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{\Dopen Z\cap\Inv(N)}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{open seg.})\\[2pt]
\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{\Dbd Z\cap\Inv(N)}{\obligations}{D} & (\text{boundary})
\end{array}}
\quad  \shortstack{$\Gamma$ \text{ saturated }\\ \text { and covered}}
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{Delay}\\ \ANDn }
\]

The two children carry the same demands and differ only in their zone. Every guard is tested against the zone, and a guard can hold throughout an
open segment yet fail at the integer point bounding it --- $x_\mu\in[1,2)$ is non-empty
on $x_\mu\in(1,2)$ and empty on $x_\mu=2$.

%The goals are carried unchanged across a delay: a delay does not change location, and the pursuit residuals in $\Gamma$ are obligations of the run, not of a single configuration, so they must survive it.
%Saturation is re-run at each delay successor, since the narrowed zone may newly satisfy or falsify a guard.

\ruleref{EX} takes the discrete steps and discharges the existential markers, one child per demanded
$\EX\psi$, each carrying $\psi$ under the full context:
\[
\ \frac{\tup{\Gamma}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}}
{\big(\ \tup{\{\psi\}\cup\Ctx}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}\ \big)_{\EX\psi\in\Gamma}}
\quad  \shortstack{$\Gamma$ \text{ saturated }\\ \text { and covered}}
\shortstack{\ruleanchor{EX}\\ \ANDn }
\]
When no $\EX\psi\in\Gamma$, a single \emph{stutter} child
$\tup{\Ctx}{\Ctx}{Z}{\obligations}{D}$ keeps the run alive. Each child inherits $\Ctx$ ---
this is where \ruleref{AX} takes effect, and it is why universal obligations constrain the whole
branching rather than one path. A pursuit's residual $\nxQ\mu^\bullet$ rides both kinds of successor:
existentially to one ($\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{E}$, the marking's choice), universally to all
($\mathrm{Q}=\mathrm{A}$, via the context). So $x_\mu$ advances by elapsing time as well as across edges.
A timed window is therefore entered by delay, its in-window stop taken at the first successor that
satisfies it; a pure-delay witness --- waiting in one location until $x_\mu\in\kI$, then stopping --- is a
chain of time-successors ending in a stop, the stutter step supplying a discrete anchor only when the
location must be left.

\subsection{Acceptance}\label{ss:loop}

A \emph{marking} $\mathcal M$ selects one child at each OR-node and \emph{all} children at each
AND-node. A \emph{candidate loop} of $\mathcal M$ is a segment $N_i,\dots,N_j$ with
$N_i\equiv N_j$, that is, equal componentwise with zones modulo $\cong_M$. It is
\emph{accepting} iff
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(i)] every until pursuit $\mu^\bullet$ active at $N_i$ is discharged on the segment:
    the $(\text{stop})$ branch of \ruleref{Uunf} is taken for $\mu$ at some $N_k$ with
    $Z_k\cap(x_\mu\in\kI)\neq\emptyset$; and
  \item[(ii)] the loop is \emph{time-progressing}: it elapses positive time, and every clock
    is either reset on the segment or extrapolated above $M$.
\end{enumerate}

Condition~(i) enforces the least-fixpoint semantics of until; condition~(ii) excludes Zeno
cycles, as a loop pinned to $x<1$ on a never-reset clock could elapse only finite total time.
Both are decidable on the segment alone, which is what makes acceptance effective: the
depth-first search never unfolds a branch past a repeated node, so every infinite path closes
into a loop, and a repeat either closes the cycle with a back-edge or closes $N_j$ according
to the test.

The marking is \emph{successful} if it contains no closed node and every candidate loop in it
is accepting, and the tableau \emph{succeeds} on $\varphi$ if the root admits a successful
marking.



